Subject: [PATCH] feat: add worker blueprints with runtime integration

X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: enable reusable worker blueprints with coordinator
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-feature
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: 0002-candidate-add-hook-context-and-post-tool-transform.patch, 1001-personal-provider-add-typed-openrouter-eu-provider.patch
X-Jcode-Patch-File: 1003-personal-feature-add-worker-blueprints-with-runtime-integration.patch

Patch intent:
Add a worker blueprint system that lets a jcode user define reusable,
specialized subagent roles in Markdown. Each blueprint supplies durable
role instructions, model, effort, enabled-tools, disabled-tools,
communication-policy, and timeout. At runtime, every valid blueprint
generates a coordinator-visible tool named swarm_<id> that spawns a
blueprint-backed worker, waits for completion, and returns a compact
summary with paginated follow-up reads. Workers submit that completion report as
their final assistant response, never through an unavailable swarm report tool.
A reserved coordinator.md file
supplies the coordinator's durable prompt and tool policy
(enabled-tools/disabled-tools), configured via the shared
[agents.worker_blueprints.coordinator] override namespace. The old
allowed-tools key is replaced by enabled-tools and disabled-tools,
matching the global [tools] policy vocabulary.

The selected blueprint name and enabled/disabled tool policy are persisted
in session snapshots, startup projections, journals, and forks. Agents own
the runtime policy registration for their lifetime. Visible and headless
spawn, session restoration, wake, and revival restore the saved policy before
worker execution, without expanding authority or restoring obsolete tools.

Why it exists:
Worker blueprints let a user specialize subagents without code changes.
A blueprint can pin a cheap model for routine work, restrict its tool
access, control its communication, and cap its runtime. The generated
tool returns a bounded summary (2000 chars) so the coordinator does not
burn tokens reading the worker's full output. A report over the limit is
shown as head and tail around an elision marker that names the omitted
char range and the follow-up offset for it, so conclusions at the end stay
visible. A deterministic follow-up read (no LLM) pages the same final
report by default, so the marker's offsets index exactly the omitted text
and never the worker prompt. scope "transcript" instead pages the whole
worker conversation newest-first (offset 0 is the end), and every page
states its scope and direction. The coordinator.md file lets a user define the
coordinator's own prompt and tool policy without installing content into
a global prompt overlay. The reserved coordinator ID uses the same
[agents.worker_blueprints.<id>] override namespace as ordinary workers,
accepting only enabled and disabled (not model, effort, or
timeout-minutes). When the swarm feature is off, swarm and generated
swarm_<id> tools are omitted from the session tool surface and rejected
at execution, the root coordinator has no coordinator-specific tool limit
or prompt applied, and swarm-specific agent settings are omitted from
the config summary. Runtime toggles remove and restore that coordinator
policy and prompt while preserving global tool limits.

